{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1LnafNYLypRLzQQNZSe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up. I did not modify `/work/putnam_2002_b6.lean`, and it still contains the `sorry`.\n\nThe statement is true, but a Lean proof needs a substantial formalization that I did not complete:\n\n1. **Reduce mod p.** Map coefficients into `ZMod p` with `MvPolynomial.map`. Coefficientwise `Int.ModEq` then becomes equality in `MvPolynomial (Fin 3) (ZMod p)`, and `det` commutes with ring homomorphisms.\n2. **Identify the factorization.** Over `F_p`, the determinant is the Moore determinant. It equals `x · ∏_{a}(y + a·x) · ∏_{b,c}(z + b·y + c·x)`, with `a, b, c ∈ {0,…,p-1}`. That has `1 + p + p²` linear factors, matching the degree of the determinant. Take `S` to be these linear forms with integer coefficients.\n3. **Prove the Moore identity in Lean.** The standard argument is an induction using the fact that a polynomial in `z` of degree `p²` vanishes at the `p²` linear combinations of `x` and `y`, plus a leading-coefficient comparison. Formalizing root-counting and divisibility in multivariate polynomial rings is the hard part.\n4. **Show the chosen linear forms are distinct.** The `Finset` product only equals the full product if the forms are pairwise distinct as integer polynomials, which requires extracting coefficients.\n\nSteps 1 and 4 are routine but tedious. Step 3 is the real obstacle, and I did not find a shortcut that avoids it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":706,"cache_read_input_tokens":1922,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":706,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"09a9e478-45e2-41dd-8cc3-c63279a034c0","uu